{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"WAVE 1 - CONFOUNDS, all recorded rather than corrected. (1) PRIOR-REVIEW EXPOSURE, the largest: every plan was authored against rebar's own criteria and pre-hardened by earlier rebar reviews (median 4, max 15), so rebar is scored on finding defects in plans it already cleaned while the other 3 arms read them blind. 2127-348c-PASS at 8 prior reviews yields rebar 0 TP. On 2948-48e5-BLOCK, the ONLY run with prior_reviews=0, rebar scored 10 TP / 0 FP - perfect precision on the single fair read in the study. (2) DET-ONLY runs: 17 of 220 candidates have metrics.llm_calls==0, rebar blocking at its deterministic precheck without any content review; all are BLOCK, reported separately and never pooled. (3) INDETERMINATE is PASS-only: 181 findings, 20% of rebar's PASS output, 0 on any BLOCK run; never scored FP per METHODOLOGY but surfaced as contradicted_unrated (7). (4) mini's step budget is not uniform (150 steps for most runs, 260 for one)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789000703296362001,"uuid":"c5bbe202-a15c-4ede-837c-97c54aef4946"}